PRIORITY QUEUE
==============

WHAT:
A queue where element with highest priority
is served first — not FIFO.

WHY/NEED:
Normal Queue serves in insertion order.
Real world needs ordering by priority —
hospital emergency, CPU scheduling,
shortest path algorithms.

HOW/EVOLUTION:
Array/LinkedList → O(n) to find min/max
Binary Heap → O(log n) insert/delete,
              O(1) peek
Hence PriorityQueue uses Binary Heap internally.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

INTERNAL — BINARY HEAP:

Conceptually a Complete Binary Tree.
Physically stored as an Array.

Min Heap → parent always smaller than children
           smallest element always at root

Max Heap → parent always larger than children
           largest element always at root

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

ARRAY REPRESENTATION:

        1
       / \
      3   2
     / \ /
    7  5 4

Array: [1, 3, 2, 7, 5, 4]
index:  0  1  2  3  4  5

PARENT-CHILD INDEX FORMULA:
parent of i      → (i-1)/2
left child of i  → 2*i+1
right child of i → 2*i+2

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

OPERATIONS:

1. INSERT — O(log n)
   a. Add new element at end of array
   b. Bubble Up (Heapify Up / Sift Up):
      - Compare with parent
      - If smaller than parent → swap
      - Repeat until root or no violation

   Example — insert 2 into [1,3,4,7,5]:
   
   Add at end → [1,3,4,7,5,2]
   
   i=5, parent=(5-1)/2=2, value=4
   2 < 4 → swap → [1,3,2,7,5,4]
   
   i=2, parent=(2-1)/2=0, value=1
   2 > 1 → stop
   
   Final: [1,3,2,7,5,4]

   Code:
   int i = size-1;
   while(i > 0){
     int parent = (i-1)/2;
     if(arr[i] < arr[parent]){
       swap(arr, i, parent);
       i = parent;
     } else break;
   }

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

2. PEEK — O(1)
   Always return arr[0] — root is always min.
   
   Code:
   return arr[0];

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

3. DELETE (remove top) — O(log n)
   a. Swap root with last element
   b. Remove last element (size--)
   c. Bubble Down (Heapify Down / Sift Down):
      - Compare with both children
      - Swap with smaller child if violated
      - Repeat until no violation or leaf

   Example — delete root from [1,3,2,7,5,4]:
   
   Step 1 — swap root with last:
   swap index 0 and index 5
   → [4,3,2,7,5,1]
   
   Step 2 — remove last:
   size-- → [4,3,2,7,5]
   
   Step 3 — bubble down 4:
   i=0, left=index1(3), right=index2(2)
   smallest=2 → swap 4 and 2
   → [2,3,4,7,5]
   
   i=2, left=index5(OOB), right=index6(OOB)
   no children → stop
   
   Final: [2,3,4,7,5]

   Code:
   swap(arr, 0, size-1);
   size--;
   int i=0;
   while(true){
     int left=2*i+1, right=2*i+2;
     int smallest=i;
     if(left<size && arr[left]<arr[smallest])
       smallest=left;
     if(right<size && arr[right]<arr[smallest])
       smallest=right;
     if(smallest!=i){
       swap(arr,i,smallest);
       i=smallest;
     } else break;
   }

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

4. BUILD HEAP FROM ARRAY — O(n)
   Instead of inserting one by one O(n log n),
   start from last non-leaf node (n/2-1)
   and bubble down each node.
   
   for(int i=n/2-1; i>=0; i--){
     bubbleDown(i);
   }

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

COMPLEXITY SUMMARY:
insert    → O(log n)
delete    → O(log n)
peek      → O(1)
buildHeap → O(n)
space     → O(n)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

JAVA USAGE:

// Min Heap (default)
PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();

// Max Heap
PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = 
    new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());

// Operations
pq.offer(val);  // insert → O(log n)
pq.poll();      // remove top → O(log n)
pq.peek();      // view top → O(1)
pq.size();      // count → O(1)
pq.isEmpty();   // check → O(1)


