INTERVIEW QUESTIONS — PRIORITY QUEUE
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q1. What is a Priority Queue and how is it 
    different from a normal Queue?

A: Normal Queue follows FIFO — first inserted 
   is first served. Priority Queue serves element
   with highest priority first regardless of 
   insertion order. In Java's default PriorityQueue,
   smallest element has highest priority (Min Heap).

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q2. What is the internal data structure of 
    Java's PriorityQueue?

A: Binary Min Heap — a complete binary tree
   stored as an array where parent is always
   smaller than its children. Smallest element
   always sits at root (index 0).

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q3. Why is Binary Heap stored as an array 
    and not as actual tree nodes?

A: Array storage avoids pointer overhead
   (no left/right node references needed).
   Parent-child relationship is derived
   mathematically:
   parent      → (i-1)/2
   left child  → 2*i+1
   right child → 2*i+2
   This gives better cache performance and
   lower memory usage than node based storage.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q4. What is the difference between 
    Min Heap and Max Heap?

A: Min Heap → parent always smaller than 
              children, smallest at root.
              Java default PriorityQueue.
   
   Max Heap → parent always larger than
              children, largest at root.
              PriorityQueue(Comparator.reverseOrder())

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q5. What is Heapify Up and when is it used?

A: Heapify Up (Bubble Up / Sift Up) is used
   during INSERT operation.
   After adding new element at end of array,
   it is compared with its parent repeatedly
   and swapped upward until heap property
   is restored.
   Time complexity → O(log n)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q6. What is Heapify Down and when is it used?

A: Heapify Down (Bubble Down / Sift Down) is
   used during DELETE operation.
   After swapping root with last element and
   removing last, new root is compared with
   both children and swapped with smaller child
   repeatedly until heap property is restored.
   Time complexity → O(log n)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q7. Why do we swap root with last element 
    during delete instead of just removing root?

A: Binary Heap must always be a complete binary
   tree — no gaps allowed. If we remove root
   directly, tree structure breaks. Swapping with
   last element maintains complete binary tree
   structure, then we restore heap property
   via Heapify Down.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q8. During Heapify Down, which child do we 
    swap with — left or right?

A: Always swap with the SMALLER child in Min Heap
   (LARGER child in Max Heap).
   Reason — swapping with larger child would
   violate heap property as larger child would
   become parent of smaller child.
   
   Code:
   int smallest = i;
   if(left<size && arr[left]<arr[smallest])
     smallest=left;
   if(right<size && arr[right]<arr[smallest])
     smallest=right;
   if(smallest!=i) swap(arr,i,smallest);

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q9. What is Build Heap and why is it O(n) 
    and not O(n log n)?

A: Build Heap converts an existing array into
   a valid heap by applying Heapify Down from
   last non-leaf node (n/2-1) to root.
   
   It is O(n) because most nodes are at bottom
   levels and need very few swaps. Only root
   needs log n swaps. Mathematically the sum
   of all swaps converges to O(n).
   
   vs inserting one by one → O(n log n)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q10. What is the difference between 
     heap memory and heap data structure?

A: Heap memory → area of RAM where Java objects
                 are dynamically allocated via
                 new keyword. Managed by GC.
   
   Heap data structure → complete binary tree
                         stored as array used
                         internally by PriorityQueue.
   
   Same word — completely different concepts.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q11. What are other types of Heaps apart 
     from Binary Heap?

A: Fibonacci Heap → used in advanced Dijkstra,
                    O(1) amortized decrease-key
   Binomial Heap  → mergeable heaps
   Ternary Heap   → 3 children, shallower tree,
                    cache optimized
   
   In practice — Binary Heap is used 99% of
   the time in interviews and production code.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q12. What is the time and space complexity 
     of PriorityQueue operations?

A: offer()     → O(log n)
   poll()      → O(log n)
   peek()      → O(1)
   size()      → O(1)
   isEmpty()   → O(1)
   buildHeap   → O(n)
   space       → O(n)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q13. Is PriorityQueue thread safe in Java?

A: No. PriorityQueue is not thread safe.
   For thread safe alternative use:
   PriorityBlockingQueue<Integer> pbq = 
     new PriorityBlockingQueue<>();

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q14. Can PriorityQueue store duplicate values?

A: Yes. PriorityQueue allows duplicate values
   unlike Set. Duplicates are treated as
   separate elements with same priority.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q15. Can PriorityQueue store null values?

A: No. PriorityQueue does not allow null.
   Throws NullPointerException if null
   is inserted. This is because comparison
   is needed for ordering and null cannot
   be compared.
